00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:19,280
[EN]  A 14-year-old autistic student begins to refuse in schools.

182
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:29,760
[EN]  If you look at only an individual, we may find sensory sensitivity, anxiety, or difficulty with sudden change.

183
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:35,640
[EN]  These may be very real, and clinical intervention may be very helpful.

184
00:16:36,360 --> 00:16:41,680
[EN]  But if you widen the lens, remember our first call here is to widen the lens.

185
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:49,580
[EN]  We may also discover bullying, misunderstanding with teachers, family conflict around attendance,

186
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:57,900
[EN]  a noisy classroom, unpredictable schedules, implicit social rules, or rigid school attendance policy.

187
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:02,860
[EN]  And then a range of interventions expands.

188
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:06,580
[EN]  Perhaps a student needs coping strategies of anxiety treatment.

189
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,940
[EN]  But perhaps a teacher needs consultation.

190
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:12,240
[EN]  Perhaps a classroom needs a quiet space.

191
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:15,340
[EN]  Perhaps instructions need to become more explicit.

192
00:17:15,340 --> 00:17:20,660
[EN]  And perhaps the school policy itself needs greater flexibility.

193
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,240
[EN]  So both a student and environmental ecology matter.

194
00:17:24,920 --> 00:17:32,740
[EN]  Community psychology often describes this as a person environment fit.

195
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:38,900
[EN*]  So with that ecological kind of framework in mind, I now want to return directly to autism itself.

196
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:48,240
[EN*]  Whitening the lens does not mean weakening diagnosis, it means asking what diagnosis can or cannot help us.

197
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:49,240
[EN*]  So here I'll give you a example.

198
00:17:49,240 --> 00:17:55,240
[EN*]  If we have a 14-year-old child, he has a problem with a problem.

199
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:58,240
[EN*]  We don't only see this child's body.

200
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:04,240
[EN*]  We may see that he's a敏感, a lot of anxiety, or he's faced with a bad change.

201
00:18:08,540 --> 00:18:11,240
[ZH] 身邊會出現被同儕霸凌的狀況

202
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:13,860
[ZH] 跟老師溝通出現了誤解

203
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:16,180
[ZH] 那還有家庭衝突

204
00:18:16,180 --> 00:18:17,500
[ZH] 或是說他受不了

205
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:19,440
[ZH] 就是一個很吵雜的教室環境

206
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:22,820
[ZH] 或是一個充滿變動的日程表

207
00:18:22,820 --> 00:18:26,880
[ZH] 還有就是可能不是很清楚的一些社會規範

208
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:35,400
[ZH] 或是一個就是很固定的一個學校出勤的政策

209
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:39,280
[ZH] 那如果我們把這個視角放大的話

210
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:40,360
[ZH] 我們就會發現

211
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:43,040
[ZH] 那我們的介入方式其實也可以跟著擴張

212
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:44,920
[ZH] 也許這個學生本身

213
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:48,480
[ZH] 他需要一些因應的策略來支援他的溝通

214
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:49,460
[ZH] 阻止他的焦慮

215
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:50,360
[ZH] 但是呢

216
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:52,260
[ZH] 如果把視角放大的話

217
00:18:52,260 --> 00:18:55,560
[ZH] 也許他的教室也可以做出一些調整

218
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:57,980
[ZH] 比如說這個教室需要變得安靜一些

219
00:18:57,980 --> 00:19:00,740
[ZH] 或是說就是也許給他的教導去

220
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:02,320
[ZH] 或是指令需要更清楚

221
00:19:02,320 --> 00:19:07,060
[ZH] 或是說學校的一些政策是可以稍微再做一些彈性調整的

222
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:11,040
[ZH] 所以學生本人還有他身處的這個環境都非常的重要

223
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:18,060
[ZH] 社群心理學呢會把這個關係稱之為人和環境的適配性

224
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:20,880
[ZH] 那有了這樣的環境架構的話

225
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:25,220
[ZH] 我現在等一下要繼續回去討論自閉症

226
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:31,580
[ZH] 但是呢我們把視角放大不代表就是要把整段站的這個地位去弱化掉

227
00:19:31,580 --> 00:19:37,860
[ZH] 其實他反而是在告訴我們診斷可以和不能告訴我們什麼事情

228
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:18,160
[EN]  Autistic people differ in communication, cognition, sensory experience, adaptive functioning, and a lot of support needed.

229
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:26,500
[EN]  A useful example comes from Saito and Kone's population study in Hirosaki, Japan.

230
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:31,580
[EN]  Hirosaki is the northern part of Japan.

231
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:40,700
[EN]  And among the 87 children identified with ASD, only about 11.5 percent had ASD without

232
00:20:40,700 --> 00:20:44,700
[EN]  any other assessed neurodevelopmental condition.

233
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:52,700
[EN]  Most also had ADHD, developmental coordination disorder, intellectual disability, borderline

234
00:20:52,700 --> 00:20:56,700
[EN]  intellectual functioning, or some combinations thereof.

235
00:20:56,700 --> 00:21:03,700
[EN]  So even when two children have the same autism diagnosis, their developmental profiles may be quite different.

236
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:12,560
[ZH] 如果有疾病的疾病,他們的疾病不介意,也不一定的全生命。

237
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:16,220
[ZH] 所以自閉症在臨床上是一個有意義的診斷

238
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:20,880
[ZH] 但是這個診斷並不能就是適用於描述每一個人

239
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:26,000
[ZH] 那自閉症患者他們在就是不同的溝通認知

240
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,800
[ZH] 還有感知經驗適應功能

241
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:33,320
[ZH] 以及就是不同程度的支援下都是不同的

242
00:21:33,320 --> 00:21:37,220
[ZH] 那這邊想要引用就是日本紅前市

243
00:21:37,220 --> 00:21:40,460
[ZH] 就是五歲幼童流行病選就報告指出

244
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:44,460
[ZH] 就是在87個有診斷出ASD的孩子裡面

245
00:21:44,460 --> 00:21:49,420
[ZH] 只有11.5%的兒童沒有其他神經發育的障礙

246
00:21:49,420 --> 00:21:52,320
[ZH] 也就是說其實大部分診斷出ASD的兒童

247
00:21:52,320 --> 00:21:54,920
[ZH] 他們至少都合併於一種其他的疾病

248
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:58,060
[ZH] 比如說是智力上面的障礙

249
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:02,320
[ZH] 或是有一些就是其他的疾病

250
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:05,360
[ZH] 那所以雖然假設我們今天有兩個孩子

251
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:07,500
[ZH] 他們都有診斷出自閉症

252
00:22:07,500 --> 00:22:11,480
[ZH] 但是他們的發展輪廓會是很不一樣的

253
00:22:11,480 --> 00:22:16,680
[ZH] 那所以這些診斷他們也代表了非常不一樣的這個模式

254
00:22:16,680 --> 00:22:21,220
[ZH] 所以可以大概形容一個人的這個發展輪廓

255
00:22:21,220 --> 00:22:23,680
[ZH] 並不代表可以適用於其他人

256
00:22:23,680 --> 00:22:27,560
[ZH] 那同時這個發展輪廓也不能表達

257
00:22:27,560 --> 00:22:30,160
[ZH] 也不能代表了他完整的一生

258
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:43,200
[EN]  Now there is another interesting complication here, that word autism serves several purposes.

259
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:50,560
[EN]  It is a diagnosis, it can determine eligibility for support, it is a scientific category used

260
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:58,040
[EN]  in the research, for some people it is also a part of social identity and community membership.

261
00:22:58,040 --> 00:23:05,780
[EN]  So, Alex Zambrovsky and her colleagues asked an important question, who gets to define autism

262
00:23:05,780 --> 00:23:08,280
[EN]  and for what purpose?

263
00:23:08,280 --> 00:23:13,640
[EN]  The answer may differ depending on whether we are diagnosing, providing services, doing

264
00:23:13,640 --> 00:23:16,620
[EN]  research, or discussing identity.

265
00:23:16,620 --> 00:23:22,520
[EN]  The point here is not that one definition is necessarily correct and others are wrong.

266
00:23:22,520 --> 00:23:28,520
[EN]  The point is that the same word can serve several different functions.

267
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:35,420
[ZH] 意義或是目的在

268
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:38,420
[ZH] 那自閉這兩字他可以是一個診斷

269
00:23:38,420 --> 00:23:44,420
[ZH] 那他也可以是用來尋求協助的一個權利

270
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:47,420
[ZH] 那他也可以是一個科學上面的分類

271
00:23:47,420 --> 00:23:49,420
[ZH] 那對於有些人來說

272
00:23:49,420 --> 00:23:52,420
[ZH] 他可以是社會上的一個身份

273
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:56,420
[ZH] 或是他會是一個社群的歸屬感

274
00:23:56,420 --> 00:23:59,420
[ZH] 所以Alexandrovsky醫師

275
00:23:59,420 --> 00:24:01,420
[ZH] 他這個研究者

276
00:24:01,420 --> 00:24:03,420
[ZH] 他就問了一個很重要的問題

277
00:24:03,420 --> 00:24:07,180
[ZH] 就是是誰有權利來定義自閉症

278
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:09,220
[ZH] 然後呢為什麼要來定義它

279
00:24:09,220 --> 00:24:12,020
[ZH] 那這個答案呢可能會有所不同

280
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:14,720
[ZH] 就是根據就是我們是要再做診斷呢

281
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:17,720
[ZH] 還是要提供服務還是要做學術研究

282
00:24:17,720 --> 00:24:22,320
[ZH] 還是要再討論社會上面的這個身份

283
00:24:22,320 --> 00:24:27,620
[ZH] 那每一個定義其實都是不一樣的

284
00:24:27,620 --> 00:24:29,820
[ZH] 那沒有辦法說誰對或誰錯

285
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:32,020
[ZH] 那重點是同樣一個字眼

286
00:24:44,220 --> 00:24:48,100
[EN*]  Neurodiversity does not have to mean no treatment.

287
00:24:48,100 --> 00:24:55,940
[EN*]  Clinical care can reduce suffering, address co-occurring conditions, support communication, and function.

288
00:24:56,900 --> 00:25:07,480
[EN*]  Neurodiversity perspective reminds us to respect neurological differences, protect dignity and agency, and avoid unnecessary normalization.

289
00:25:08,540 --> 00:25:15,700
[EN]  Community psychology adds attention to the person environment fit, the person environment fit that I was talking about.

290
00:25:16,300 --> 00:25:21,300
[EN]  participation, belonging, and prevention of secondary harm.

291
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:25,320
[EN]  Now these perspectives do not need to compete.

292
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:28,720
[EN]  They can complement one another.

293
00:25:28,720 --> 00:25:32,300
[EN]  The phrase that I'll be using just a little bit later

294
00:25:33,020 --> 00:25:37,020
[ZH] 複製作的避免運動,這是一個很大的詞。

295
00:25:37,020 --> 00:25:44,020
[ZH] 避免運動並不必須要從一個人向環境。

296
00:25:44,020 --> 00:25:48,020
[ZH] 今天的研究會主題是神經多樣性。

297
00:25:48,020 --> 00:25:53,020
[ZH] 當我們談到神經多樣性的話,其實有一個很重要的一題我想要強調,

298
00:25:53,020 --> 00:25:56,740
[ZH] 神經多樣性不代表完全不需要治療介入

299
00:25:56,740 --> 00:26:02,380
[ZH] 那比如說臨床的照護就可以幫助這個當事人減輕痛苦

300
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:05,000
[ZH] 支援他溝通還有日常適應功能

301
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:10,240
[ZH] 還有神經多樣性這個觀念本身就在提醒我們說

302
00:26:10,240 --> 00:26:15,720
[ZH] 我們對於神經可能發展和結構很不一樣的人應該給予尊重

303
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:20,160
[ZH] 這樣就可以確保他人的尊嚴還有自主權

304
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:23,060
[ZH] 然後也可以避免不必要的正常化

305
00:26:23,060 --> 00:26:24,500
[ZH] 那社群心理學呢

306
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:27,300
[ZH] 還另外再增加了一些關注的焦點

307
00:26:27,300 --> 00:26:29,960
[ZH] 比如說人與環境的適配性

308
00:26:29,960 --> 00:26:33,360
[ZH] 還有改變個人的時候也同時改變環境

309
00:26:33,360 --> 00:26:35,400
[ZH] 那這些觀點並不是互相排斥的

310
00:26:35,400 --> 00:26:37,820
[ZH] 他們其實是互相支援補充的

311
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:39,660
[ZH] 那我等一下會講到一個詞

312
00:26:39,660 --> 00:26:42,820
[ZH] 就是雙向調適

313
00:26:42,820 --> 00:26:46,920
[ZH] 就是其實適應這件事情並不是單向的

314
00:26:46,920 --> 00:26:49,400
[ZH] 並不是這個人去適應他的環境

315
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:52,500
[ZH] 其實環境也可以反過來適應這個人

316
00:26:56,500 --> 00:26:59,580
[ZH] 然後我們需要認識這些認識

317
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:05,680
[EN]  What is expected timing of conversation?

318
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:08,600
[EN]  How much emotional expression is considered appropriate?

319
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:13,920
[EN]  How much independence is expected?

320
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:16,720
[EN]  How much conformity to group routines?

321
00:27:16,720 --> 00:27:19,220
[EN]  These norms matter because differences

322
00:27:19,220 --> 00:27:22,320
[EN]  are not simply observed, they are interpretive.

323
00:27:23,500 --> 00:27:26,500
[EN]  Behavior may be seen as rude, immature,

324
00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:29,780
[EN]  uncooperative, unusual, or disordered,

325
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:34,780
[EN]  depending on the city and the social expectations operating there.

326
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:42,780
[EN]  And then another question appears, is a combination possible, or is a person expected to hide the difference?

327
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:51,780
[EN]  Culture does not create autism, but social norms can influence whether a neurological difference becomes a barrier.

328
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:56,780
[EN]  Now, so far I've been talking about diagnosis, context, and social norms.

329
00:27:56,780 --> 00:28:03,400
[EN]  I now want to shift toward the lived experience, especially some recent work from Japan.

330
00:28:00,460 --> 00:28:03,460
[ZH] 尤其是日本的一些最近的工作。

331
00:28:17,460 --> 00:28:19,260
[ZH] 是正常和適當的

332
00:28:19,260 --> 00:28:23,560
[ZH] 然後還有多少的獨立和相互依賴是恰當的

333
00:28:23,560 --> 00:28:25,960
[ZH] 怎麼樣才叫做遵守團體規範

334
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:28,560
[ZH] 其實這些所謂的社會規範

335
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:32,660
[ZH] 他們都是每個人解讀的方式不一樣

336
00:28:32,660 --> 00:28:34,960
[ZH] 所以在一個環境中有一個行為

337
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:37,460
[ZH] 可能會被視為是粗乳不成熟

338
00:28:37,460 --> 00:28:38,760
[ZH] 非常不禮貌的

339
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:41,760
[ZH] 但是來到另外一個環境當中

340
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:45,160
[ZH] 也許會有不一樣的社會期待值

341
00:28:45,160 --> 00:28:47,920
[ZH] 所以這邊就領明到另外一個問題

342
00:28:47,920 --> 00:28:50,520
[ZH] 就是我們是否可以做出調整

343
00:28:50,520 --> 00:28:56,360
[ZH] 還是說我們會要求當事人要去隱藏這個與他人不一樣的地方

344
00:28:56,360 --> 00:28:58,960
[ZH] 文化本身並不會創造出自閉

345
00:28:58,960 --> 00:29:02,880
[ZH] 但是社會規範強加在別人身上的話

346
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:06,780
[EN*]  It is possible to make the same change in the world.

347
00:29:06,780 --> 00:29:12,780
[EN*]  So I will turn to the conversation about the experience of the life of the world.

348
00:29:12,780 --> 00:29:16,780
[EN*]  I will use some of the research in the recent research.

349
00:29:17,780 --> 00:29:30,780
[EN*]  Now, Dr. Harada and his colleagues interviewed seven late-diagnosed autistic adults in Japan.

350
00:29:30,780 --> 00:29:36,780
[EN]  Now, several themes emerged out of their discussions or interviews.

351
00:29:36,780 --> 00:29:43,780
[EN]  Participants described feeling different, sometimes for many years, without necessarily understanding why.

352
00:29:43,780 --> 00:29:53,400
[EN]  why. They describe misunderstanding, bullying, stigma, and social difficulty. Diagnosis sometimes

353
00:29:53,400 --> 00:30:00,960
[EN]  brought relief because it offers an explanation. But diagnosis could also bring uncertainty

354
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:20,320
[ZH] 不一定的感受和混亂感受。重要的是,所要的參與者並非只是有更好的社交技術,他們也想認識、認識、信心和信心。這意味著,所要的人的需要,並非從診斥者的診斥者不一定會顯示。

355
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:33,040
[ZH] 那參與面談的這些自閉診者

356
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:35,400
[ZH] 他們描述他們的感受

357
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:39,800
[ZH] 他說他小時候就感覺到自己是從小跟人家不一樣

358
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:42,000
[ZH] 但是他始終都不知道原因

359
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,040
[ZH] 那他們也遭遇了就是長期被誤解啊

360
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:47,840
[ZH] 霸凌啊汙名化還有人際誤解等等

361
00:30:47,840 --> 00:30:51,600
[ZH] 那就是當他們獲得診斷以後

362
00:30:51,600 --> 00:30:54,640
[ZH] 他們會覺得恍然大悟釋懷的感覺

363
00:30:54,640 --> 00:30:56,320
[ZH] 但同時也覺得很複雜

364
00:30:56,320 --> 00:30:59,120
[ZH] 就是情感上面覺得好像

365
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:02,240
[ZH] 這個診斷也帶來了一些不確定感

